  "success": true,
  "output": "## Performance Analysis — Canon\n\nScope: `web/` is implemented (Next.js console); `engine/` is still stub code (every pipeline function in `blocking.py`, `detect.py`, `graph.py`, `db.py` raises `NotImplementedError` — Phase 2/3 per `AGENTS.md`'s build order). So the real findings are in `web/`; engine notes below are design-risk flags for when that code gets written.\n\nOverall: the web layer is unusually careful about the things this prompt asks about — `getQueuePage` already batches counts/rows/child-counts into 3 parallel round trips instead of per-row queries, `getDb()` caches the connection on `globalThis` across HMR reloads, and every effect I checked (`GradientWaves`, `RunLive`, `LogoLoop`, `ConflictFocus`) cleans up correctly. So instead of manufacturing issues in code that's already solid, here's what's actually there.\n\n### 1. N+1 query patterns — none in the query code, but the schema is missing the indexes that make the batching actually pay off\n\n`conflicts` (`web/lib/db/schema.ts:137`) has **no index at all** beyond its primary key. Every conflict screen filters and sorts on it:\n\n- `getQueuePage` → `WHERE conflicts.run_id = ? ORDER BY conflicts.severity DESC`\n- `getSeverityBreakdown`, `countConflicts`, `getQueueCounts` → `WHERE conflicts.run_id = ? GROUP BY severity`\n- `loadDecisions`, `loadOrphans` (export) → same\n\nRight now this is invisible because the synthetic dataset is small, but at the scale `AGENTS.md` targets (500 accounts × the injection rates in the eval spec → thousands of conflict rows per run), every one of those well-batched queries becomes a full sequential scan. Same gap on `resolutions.conflict_id` (used in every `LEFT JOIN`, and already flagged in a comment in `conflicts.ts:64` as needing a unique index — that's a correctness *and* a performance gap) and on `audit_log.run_id` (used by `listRunAudit`, `getAuditPage`).\n\n```ts\n// web/lib/db/schema.ts\nexport const conflicts = pgTable(\"conflicts\", {\n  // ...unchanged columns\n}, (table) => [\n  index(\"conflicts_run_severity_idx\").on(table.runId, table.severity),\n]);\n\nexport const resolutions = pgTable(\"resolutions\", {\n  // ...unchanged columns\n}, (table) => [\n  uniqueIndex(\"resolutions_conflict_id_uq\").on(table.conflictId), // also fixes the dup-join risk noted in conflicts.ts\n]);\n\nexport const auditLog = pgTable(\"audit_log\", {\n  // ...unchanged columns\n}, (table) => [\n  index(\"audit_log_run_created_idx\").on(table.runId, table.createdAt),\n]);\n```\nRun `npx drizzle-kit generate && npx drizzle-kit migrate` after.\n\n**Engine risk to watch for when Phase 2/3 land:** `db.py`'s `write_entities/write_matches/write_conflicts` already take `list[...]` (good — signals bulk insert intent). When implemented, make sure they use `executemany`/`COPY`, not a Python loop calling `INSERT` per row — that's the classic engine-side N+1 for a pipeline writing thousands of rows per run.\n\n### 2. Unnecessary React re-renders\n\n`ConflictQueue` (`web/components/conflicts/conflict-queue.tsx:57-63`) creates a new closure per row on every render:\n\n```ts\nconst submitFor = useCallback(\n  (conflictId: string): ReviewSubmit => (action) => submit(conflictId, action),\n  [submit],\n);\n// ...\nsubmit={submitFor(row.conflictId)}   // new function identity every render, for every row\n```\n\nNone of `ConflictDiffRow` / `ConflictDenseTable` / `ConflictFocus`'s row components are `React.memo`'d, so today this is inert — the whole list re-renders on any `selectedIndex` change (e.g. every `j`/`k` keypress) regardless. That's the actual issue: pressing `j` in a 100-row ledger re-renders all 100 `ConflictDiffRow`s instead of just the two rows whose `selected` prop flipped.\n\nFix — memo the row and stabilize the callback so memo isn't defeated:\n\n```tsx\n// conflict-diff-row.tsx\nexport const ConflictDiffRow = React.memo(function ConflictDiffRow({ row, submit, pending, ... }) {\n  // ...unchanged\n});\n```\n```ts\n// conflict-queue.tsx — one stable submit per conflictId instead of a factory called at render time\nconst submitById = useMemo(() => {\n  const cache = new Map<string, ReviewSubmit>();\n  return (conflictId: string) => {\n    let fn = cache.get(conflictId);\n    if (!fn) {\n      fn = (action) => submit(conflictId, action);\n      cache.set(conflictId, fn);\n    }\n    return fn;\n  };\n}, [submit]);\n```\nWith 100 rows this isn't costly today, but it's the reason a future attempt to `memo` these components (a natural next step once the queue grows) will silently fail to help.\n\nMinor: `TextType` (`web/components/visual/text-type.tsx:101`) recomputes `phrases.reduce(...)` to find the widest phrase on every tick of the typing animation (every 30–90ms while typing/deleting). `phrases` is static per mount, so:\n```ts\nconst widest = useMemo(\n  () => phrases.reduce((a, b) => (b.length > a.length ? b : a), \"\"),\n  [phrases],\n);\n```\nCheap either way given the array size — flagging it because it's a clean example of a computation re-running on a fast render cycle for no reason, not because it's costing anything measurable.\n\n### 3. Caching opportunities\n\n`requireReviewer()` (`web/lib/server/auth.ts:44-57`) calls `auth.protect()`, `auth()`, and `currentUser()` on **every** protected Server Component render and route handler — and `currentUser()` is a live round trip to Clerk's Backend API each time (Clerk's own `cache()` wrapper only dedupes calls *within* one request, not across navigations). Given `requireReviewer`/`getReviewer` gate essentially every console page, that's a Clerk API call on every page load.\n\nIf the reviewer label (`reviewerLabel(user, userId)` in `lib/reviewer-label.ts`) only needs name/email, check whether those are already present in the session token's `sessionClaims` — if so, read from `auth()`'s claims instead of calling `currentUser()`, and reserve the backend fetch for the rare case a claim is missing:\n\n```ts\nexport async function requireReviewer(): Promise<Reviewer> {\n  await auth.protect();\n  const { userId, sessionClaims } = await auth();\n  if (!userId) throw new Error(\"Authenticated session carried no userId.\");\n\n  const label = reviewerLabelFromClaims(sessionClaims) ?? reviewerLabel(await currentUser(), userId);\n  return { userId, label };\n}\n```\nThis trades a guaranteed backend call for a JWT claim read on the common path — worth confirming your Clerk instance's default claims actually carry what `reviewerLabel` needs before making the change.\n\nNothing else jumped out as a caching gap — Server Components here are correctly doing per-request DB reads (that's the intended model per `AGENTS.md`'s \"Server Components by default\" and TanStack Query is deliberately scoped to just the run-status poll), and `getDb()` already caches the pooled connection across HMR reloads.\n\n### 4. Memory leaks\n\nChecked every component with `useEffect`/timers/observers/listeners (`RunLive`, `GradientWaves`, `LogoLoop`, `TextType`, `ConflictFocus`, `ConflictQueue`). All clean:\n- `GradientWaves` cancels its rAF loop, disconnects its `ResizeObserver`, removes the `webglcontextlost` listener, and explicitly releases the WebGL context on unmount.\n- `RunLive`'s polling stops via TanStack Query's `refetchInterval` returning `false` on terminal status, and the `Elapsed` sub-clock's `setInterval` is cleared on unmount/`running` change.\n- `LogoLoop`'s `ResizeObserver` is disconnected on unmount.\n\nNo leaks found — nothing to fix here.\n\n### 5. Redundant computations\n\nNothing significant beyond the two already noted above (`TextType`'s `widest`, and the `submitFor` closures). `diffSegments` (`lib/diff-chars.ts`) recomputes on every render of `DiffValue` rather than being memoized, but it's O(min(len_a, len_b)) over short field values (account names, ids) — not worth the memoization overhead.\n\n---\n\n**Priority order if you want to act on this now:** the missing indexes (§1) are the one item that will actually bite once you run the eval harness at 500+ accounts — everything else is either inert today (`submitFor`) or genuinely clean. I'd hold off on the row-memoization change until the queue is real data at scale, since premature `memo` on components that re-render fine at today's size is exactly the kind of abstraction `AGENTS.md` tells you not to add.\n",
